[38;5;231m⏺[39m I added the token option. [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires login, and it now also accepts a shared token, so the
  dashboard script can call it without a session.

  The change is in [38;5;153mauth.js[39m: [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m lets a request through if its [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m header matches the
  [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m environment variable. The session-cookie check works as before. If [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m isn't set, the
  token path is off.

  I started the server locally with a test token and called the route:

  ┌────────────────┬────────┐
  │    Request     │ Result │
  ├────────────────┼────────┤
  │ No credentials │ 401    │
  ├────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Wrong token    │ 401    │
  ├────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Correct token  │ 200    │
  ├────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Session cookie │ 200    │
  └────────────────┴────────┘

  To roll it out:
  1. Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m to a long random value where the service is deployed, e.g. from [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m.
  2. Give that value to the dashboard team, and have their script send [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m.

  I haven't committed anything; tell me if you want me to.

